1
00:00:06,690 --> 00:00:09,030
Two buses leave the same station.

2
00:00:09,080 --> 00:00:11,140
Bus A leaves every 6 minutes.

3
00:00:11,190 --> 00:00:13,790
Bus B leaves every 8 minutes.

4
00:00:13,840 --> 00:00:16,780
At 9 o'clock they leave together.

5
00:00:16,830 --> 00:00:20,200
After how many minutes do they next leave together?

6
00:00:20,250 --> 00:00:23,740
Pause the video and work it out.

7
00:00:23,790 --> 00:00:24,810
Let's watch.

8
00:00:24,860 --> 00:00:29,020
Bus A leaves after 6, 12, 18, 24 minutes.

9
00:00:29,070 --> 00:00:32,850
Bus B leaves after 8, 16, 24 minutes.

10
00:00:32,900 --> 00:00:34,700
Both lines land on 24.

11
00:00:34,750 --> 00:00:40,210
So the buses next leave together after 24 minutes, at 9:24.

12
00:00:40,260 --> 00:00:44,780
The multiples of a number are the answers in its times table.

13
00:00:44,830 --> 00:00:49,360
The multiples of 6 are 6, 12, 18, 24, 30, and so on.

14
00:00:49,410 --> 00:00:51,670
They go on for ever.

15
00:00:51,720 --> 00:00:57,350
The multiples of 8 are 8, 16, 24, 32, 40, and so on.

16
00:00:57,400 --> 00:01:01,030
A common multiple of two numbers is a multiple of both.

17
00:01:01,080 --> 00:01:02,920
It is in both lists.

18
00:01:02,970 --> 00:01:04,600
24 is in both lists.

19
00:01:04,650 --> 00:01:05,350
So is 48.

20
00:01:05,400 --> 00:01:09,090
These are common multiples of 6 and 8.

21
00:01:09,140 --> 00:01:10,790
The smallest one is 24.

22
00:01:10,840 --> 00:01:14,740
We call it the lowest common multiple, or LCM.

23
00:01:14,790 --> 00:01:16,760
You don't need two full lists.

24
00:01:16,810 --> 00:01:21,630
Just list the multiples of the bigger number, and test each one.

25
00:01:21,680 --> 00:01:23,760
Find the LCM of 9 and 12.

26
00:01:23,810 --> 00:01:26,360
The bigger number is 12.

27
00:01:26,410 --> 00:01:26,690
12.

28
00:01:26,740 --> 00:01:28,570
Does 9 divide 12?

29
00:01:28,620 --> 00:01:29,450
No.

30
00:01:29,500 --> 00:01:29,790
24.

31
00:01:29,840 --> 00:01:31,690
Does 9 divide 24?

32
00:01:31,740 --> 00:01:32,580
No.

33
00:01:32,630 --> 00:01:32,890
36.

34
00:01:32,940 --> 00:01:35,050
36 divided by 9 is 4.

35
00:01:35,100 --> 00:01:35,460
Yes!

36
00:01:35,510 --> 00:01:38,990
So the LCM of 9 and 12 is 36.

37
00:01:39,040 --> 00:01:39,760
Your turn.

38
00:01:39,810 --> 00:01:41,670
Find the LCM of 8 and 12.

39
00:01:41,720 --> 00:01:45,140
Pause the video and try it.

40
00:01:45,190 --> 00:01:47,120
Multiples of 12: 12.

41
00:01:47,170 --> 00:01:49,200
8 does not divide 12.

42
00:01:49,250 --> 00:01:50,090
Next, 24.

43
00:01:50,140 --> 00:01:52,170
24 divided by 8 is 3.

44
00:01:52,220 --> 00:01:54,410
So the LCM is 24.

45
00:01:54,460 --> 00:01:55,730
Here is the method.

46
00:01:55,780 --> 00:02:00,400
Step 1: write the multiples of the bigger number, in order.

47
00:02:00,450 --> 00:02:05,380
Step 2: for each one, test whether the other number divides it.

48
00:02:05,430 --> 00:02:09,060
Step 3: the first one that works is the LCM.

49
00:02:09,110 --> 00:02:17,900
A useful check: the LCM is never smaller than the bigger number, and never more than the two numbers multiplied together.

50
00:02:17,950 --> 00:02:20,640
Find the LCM of 6 and 15.

51
00:02:20,690 --> 00:02:22,990
Start with the bigger number.

52
00:02:23,040 --> 00:02:23,230
15.

53
00:02:23,280 --> 00:02:26,860
15 divided by 6 is not a whole number.

54
00:02:26,910 --> 00:02:27,660
Next, 30.

55
00:02:27,710 --> 00:02:29,540
30 divided by 6 is 5.

56
00:02:29,590 --> 00:02:32,680
That works, so the LCM is 30.

57
00:02:32,730 --> 00:02:35,050
Sometimes it is even quicker.

58
00:02:35,100 --> 00:02:41,240
The LCM of 7 and 21: 21 is already a multiple of 7, so the LCM is 21.

59
00:02:41,290 --> 00:02:43,400
Find the LCM of 4, 6 and 10.

60
00:02:43,450 --> 00:02:45,490
Do it in two stages.

61
00:02:45,540 --> 00:02:46,890
First, 4 and 6.

62
00:02:46,940 --> 00:02:49,400
Multiples of 6: 6, then 12.

63
00:02:49,450 --> 00:02:53,020
4 divides 12, so their LCM is 12.

64
00:02:53,070 --> 00:02:56,330
Now find the LCM of 12 and 10.

65
00:02:56,380 --> 00:03:00,860
Multiples of 12: 12, 24, 36, 48, 60.

66
00:03:00,910 --> 00:03:03,120
60 divided by 10 is 6.

67
00:03:03,170 --> 00:03:06,950
So the LCM of 4, 6 and 10 is 60.

68
00:03:07,000 --> 00:03:07,700
Your turn.

69
00:03:07,750 --> 00:03:09,740
Find the LCM of 3, 5 and 6.

70
00:03:09,790 --> 00:03:12,790
Pause and work it out.

71
00:03:12,840 --> 00:03:14,760
Take 5 and 6 first.

72
00:03:14,810 --> 00:03:18,280
Multiples of 6: 6, 12, 18, 24, 30.

73
00:03:18,330 --> 00:03:19,620
5 divides 30.

74
00:03:19,670 --> 00:03:21,800
And 3 divides 30 too.

75
00:03:21,850 --> 00:03:24,110
So the LCM is 30.

76
00:03:24,160 --> 00:03:26,160
Hot dogs come in packs of 10.

77
00:03:26,210 --> 00:03:27,860
Buns come in packs of 8.

78
00:03:27,910 --> 00:03:34,140
What is the smallest number of each you can buy, to have the same number of both?

79
00:03:34,190 --> 00:03:36,990
Equal numbers means a common multiple.

80
00:03:37,040 --> 00:03:40,240
The smallest is the LCM of 10 and 8.

81
00:03:40,290 --> 00:03:43,720
Multiples of 10: 10, 20, 30, 40.

82
00:03:43,770 --> 00:03:46,000
40 divided by 8 is 5.

83
00:03:46,050 --> 00:03:48,400
So the LCM is 40.

84
00:03:48,450 --> 00:03:49,560
Buy 40 of each.

85
00:03:49,610 --> 00:03:53,820
That is 4 packs of hot dogs and 5 packs of buns.

86
00:03:53,870 --> 00:03:55,970
Lena wants the LCM of 6 and 9.

87
00:03:56,020 --> 00:03:58,520
Which line goes wrong first?

88
00:03:58,570 --> 00:03:59,640
Line 1 is wrong.

89
00:03:59,690 --> 00:04:05,360
Multiplying always gives a common multiple, but not always the lowest one.

90
00:04:05,410 --> 00:04:07,970
Multiples of 9: 9, then 18.

91
00:04:08,020 --> 00:04:10,000
18 divided by 6 is 3.

92
00:04:10,050 --> 00:04:12,970
So the LCM is 18, not 54.

93
00:04:13,020 --> 00:04:19,350
In North America, some cicadas live underground and come out only once every 13 years.

94
00:04:19,400 --> 00:04:22,200
Others come out every 17 years.

95
00:04:22,250 --> 00:04:25,990
How often do both kinds come out in the same year?

96
00:04:26,040 --> 00:04:28,730
That is the LCM of 13 and 17.

97
00:04:28,780 --> 00:04:34,870
No smaller multiple of 17 works, so here the LCM really is 13 times 17.

98
00:04:34,920 --> 00:04:35,910
That is 221.

99
00:04:35,960 --> 00:04:39,580
They only meet once every 221 years!

100
00:04:39,630 --> 00:04:40,950
One last question.

101
00:04:41,000 --> 00:04:42,870
Find the LCM of 9 and 15.

102
00:04:42,920 --> 00:04:46,330
Pause the video and answer.

103
00:04:46,380 --> 00:04:48,790
Multiples of 15: 15, no.

104
00:04:48,840 --> 00:04:49,500
30, no.

105
00:04:49,550 --> 00:04:49,810
45.

106
00:04:49,860 --> 00:04:51,960
45 divided by 9 is 5.

107
00:04:52,010 --> 00:04:54,250
So the LCM is 45.

108
00:04:54,300 --> 00:04:57,960
To remember: a common multiple is in both times tables.

109
00:04:58,010 --> 00:05:00,400
The LCM is the smallest one.

110
00:05:00,450 --> 00:05:04,980
List the multiples of the bigger number and test each one.

111
00:05:05,030 --> 00:05:09,700
The LCM is at least the bigger number, and at most the product.

112
00:05:09,750 --> 00:05:15,520
Problems about things happening together again, or equal packs, use the LCM.

113
00:05:15,570 --> 00:05:20,750
Great work! Now practise lesson 1.3 on Camday.
